comment_text:
We have a stark set of choices in front of us and I will do everything in my power to choose and support those who choose:
Humanity over Nationality;
Hope over Despair;
Justice over Injustice;
Respect over Humiliation;
Knowledge over Ignorance;
Truth over Lies;
Peace over War;
And Love over Hatred;
For all the claims about being disenfranchised and forgotten, those who voted for Trump know exactly what they were doing. And those who stayed at home, pretending false equivalencies, have let the not-so-silent minority pretend that they are the so-called silent majority. Those who voted for Trump have and will always choose the opposite of what we would and should choose. That much should be clear to everyone by now and was always clear to those among us to never fit the dominant paradigm that fit Mr. Trump's narrative of "real Americans". For the record, I am an Indian American, I am a woman, and my husband and I pay job lots of taxes and we are happy to do so.

As a gun owner and former NRA member, I find the ongoing television coverage of the shooting at Stoneman Douglas High School has become prurient voyeurism. Interviewing survivors gives a superficial image of concern and compassion; questions on what the survivors were feeling in the minutes of crisis, posed with softly voiced empathy, is only slightly more palatable than “thoughts and prayers” quotes bandied about by all the talking heads. Neither addresses the underlying problem; both allow us to feel better about ourselves while we do nothing to stop the violence.

Focus on the shooter’s access to high capacity weapons;
Address people with relevant knowledge failing to act to prevent the tragedy;
Explore why Congress prohibits adequate funding on gun violence research (interview Senator Kamala Harris, she has some excellent ideas);
Stop the tired lie that mass shootings are solely due to inadequate mental health services – it is access to high lethality weapons;
Interview our political leaders on their acceptance of campaign dollars from the NRA compared to their voting patterns on gun safety – Private sale loophole; boyfriend loophole, yada, yada, yada...

Yes, network news broadcasts are a difficult format in which to fully present complex issues. Let’s stop pretending. Television interviews of crying survivors and distraught family members, asking how they feel, is NOT news. Leave the emotional voyeurism to other formats. Address the real problem: too many guns.

Mr. Trump's choice of cabinet and staff appointees clearly indicate, that whatever his bows to populist sentiments during his campaign, his administration will end up promoting Speaker Ryan's radical right-wing agenda. Mr. Pruitt's past actions clearly serve to strengthen this belief.

Fasten your seat-belts and watch Speaker Ryan and the Trump administration proceed to:

(a) reduce taxes, especially for the "makers";

(b) privatize, otherwise "reform" or eliminate programs that benefit workers, consumers and "takers";

(c) deregulate--benefit the worthy "makers" by minimizing the environmental and financial regulations that impede their efforts to "take" ever more;

(d) in short, insure that property rights always trump all claims of basic human rights--that the system enshrines the "right" of the greediest among us to neglect the neediest among us.

Thus the GOP slogan: Ever Regressively Backward to the Laissez Faire Glories of the Gilded Age!

To empower the Republicans is to embolden all the forces of anti-democratic plutocracy and the interests of the crony capitalists.

As an Indigenous woman and a Torres Strait Islander from Australia I'm fiercely proud of cultural heritage. And yes Indigenous people in Australia do represent 3% of Australia's population and yes we do get billions of dollars in funding, but if you contrast that level funding to what's being done for the rest Australia, my guess is, it would be barely visible. In Australia, the current statistics that represent the ongoing struggle from my Aboriginal community is heartbreaking:
* The highest percentage of unemployed people in Australia are Aboriginal;
* Aboriginal people in Australia currently have a Life expectancy that's 10
years less, than any other Australian;
* The highest represented group in youth suicide rates across Australia are
young Aboriginal people - one was as young as 10 years old;
* We also have the highest incarceration rates in Australia and; that's now
increased by 88%
* And the state of health in our Aboriginal communities, well just look at our
Life expectancy rates;
And as for all that funding we get from the Government, they dictate to us how it's used and spent, we get no say in it, because if we did, I guarantee you we'd make sure employment opportunities for Aboriginal people we're higher and we'd address the current suicide rates of our young Aboriginal people. But we will continue to fight for change because the pride of our culture has sustained us as one the oldest living culture in the World.

Bringing up the voicemail of Baldwin yelling at his daughter is pretty low and reflects poorly on the author. And Dylan Farrow presents as a permanent victim. Her unsubstantiated molestation is the centerpiece of her life. There's a viciousness to Mia Farrow's decades long vendetta against Allen;
iunderpinned by a seemingly insatiable appetite for publicity.

Comparing Dylan Farrow's situation to those of Weinstein and Spacey's victims is just plain wrong.
